From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ell <guardwire2@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 21:00:00 +0300
Subject: drv:thermal:sun8i: guard against NULL caldata in calibrate functions

sun8i_h3_ths_calibrate(), sun50i_h6_ths_calibrate() and
sun55i_a523_ths_calibrate() all dereference caldata[0] as their very
first action, without checking that caldata itself is non-NULL:

    if (!caldata[0] || callen < 2 * tmdev->chip->sensor_num)
            return -EINVAL;

sun8i_h3_ths_calibrate() is also used directly as the R40 chip's
.calibrate callback (sun8i_r40_ths.calibrate), so this affects boards
beyond H3.

On a Banana Pi M2 Ultra (Allwinner R40), this crashes with a NULL
pointer Oops on every single boot:

    Unable to handle kernel NULL pointer dereference at virtual
    address 00000000 when read
    ...
    PC is at sun8i_h3_ths_calibrate+0xc/0x6c [sun8i_thermal]
    LR is at sun8i_ths_probe+0x221/0x398 [sun8i_thermal]
    ...
    Call trace:
     sun8i_h3_ths_calibrate [sun8i_thermal] from sun8i_ths_probe+0x221/0x398 [sun8i_thermal]

Practical effect on this board: no thermal_zone is ever registered
(/sys/class/thermal only shows a cooling_device, no thermal_zone0),
so there is zero temperature monitoring and no automatic thermal
throttling.

There is a second, much more serious effect. This Oops happens while
sun8i_thermal.ko is being loaded (finit_module()). The kernel's
concurrent-module-load dedup mechanism (kernel/module/main.c,
idempotent()/idempotent_complete(), added by commit 9b9879fc0327
"modules: catch concurrent module loads, treat them as idempotent")
uses a stack-allocated struct linked into a global idem_hash list
while the load is in progress. When the loading task is abnormally
terminated by an Oops mid-load, that stack-allocated node can be left
dangling in the list (this exact failure mode has been reported
upstream via KASAN/syzkaller against idempotent(), e.g. slab-use-
after-free and wild-memory-access reports against this same code
path). On this board, the corruption doesn't crash immediately
(no KASAN in a normal build) - instead a later concurrent module-load
attempt spins forever walking the corrupted list while holding
idem_lock. In practice, this reliably hangs `reboot`: the kernel's
shutdown path calls usermodehelper_disable() (kernel/reboot.c) before
device_shutdown(), which itself waits on module-loading-adjacent
locking, and gets stuck on the corrupted state left behind by this
Oops - every single time, with no dmesg output whatsoever past
"Rebooting." (not even the kernel's own "reboot: Restarting system"
line, since the hang happens earlier in kernel_restart_prepare()).

Root-caused and verified on hardware (Banana Pi M2 Ultra, Armbian
26.11 rolling, kernel 6.18.51-current-sunxi):
 - Ruled out via targeted driver unbind + reboot test: WiFi
   (brcmfmac), Bluetooth (hci_uart_bcm), the watchdog driver
   (sunxi-wdt), and cpufreq-dt are NOT the cause.
 - Booting with `initcall_debug` (which makes device_shutdown()
   print the name of every device right before calling its
   .shutdown()) showed *zero* such prints during a hung reboot,
   proving the hang happens before the per-device shutdown loop
   even starts - i.e. in usermodehelper_disable(), consistent with
   the corrupted idem_hash theory above.
 - Serial-BREAK-triggered magic SysRq gets no response during the
   hang, consistent with the stuck CPU spinning with local IRQs
   disabled inside the corrupted-list walk.
 - This exact patch, built into a full kernel with the complete
   sunxi-6.18 patch set applied, was confirmed to fix reboot on this
   board: 3/3 clean reboots with no workaround, versus 0/many
   without this fix (previously worked around with a
   /usr/lib/systemd/system-shutdown/ hook forcing `echo b >
   /proc/sysrq-trigger`, which bypasses usermodehelper_disable()
   and device_shutdown() entirely and always worked - confirming the
   platform's actual SoC reset path is fine, and the hang is purely
   this software issue).

Fix: check caldata itself (not just caldata[0]) before dereferencing
it, in all three affected calibrate functions. This is a one-line,
no-behavior-change fix for the normal (calibration data present)
path, and turns the crash into the same graceful "-EINVAL, run
uncalibrated" path already used elsewhere in this file when
calibration data is genuinely unavailable.

Signed-off-by: ell <guardwire2@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/thermal/sun8i_thermal.c | 6 +++---
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/thermal/sun8i_thermal.c b/drivers/thermal/sun8i_thermal.c
index 111111111111..222222222222 100644
--- a/drivers/thermal/sun8i_thermal.c
+++ b/drivers/thermal/sun8i_thermal.c
@@ -228,7 +228,7 @@ static int sun8i_h3_ths_calibrate(struct ths_device *tmdev,
 {
 	int i;
 
-	if (!caldata[0] || callen < 2 * tmdev->chip->sensor_num)
+	if (!caldata || !caldata[0] || callen < 2 * tmdev->chip->sensor_num)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	for (i = 0; i < tmdev->chip->sensor_num; i++) {
@@ -249,7 +249,7 @@ static int sun50i_h6_ths_calibrate(struct ths_device *tmdev,
 	struct device *dev = tmdev->dev;
 	int i, ft_temp;
 
-	if (!caldata[0])
+	if (!caldata || !caldata[0])
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	/*
@@ -343,7 +343,7 @@ static int sun55i_a523_ths_calibrate(struct ths_device *tmdev,
 	struct device *dev = tmdev->dev;
 	int i, ft_temp;
 
-	if (!caldata[0])
+	if (!caldata || !caldata[0])
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	ft_temp = (((caldata[2] << 8) | (caldata[1] >> 8)) & FT_TEMP_MASK) * 100;
-- 
